# Mi Pad 2 USB serial debug

This package exposes the tablet's USB device controller as one CDC ACM serial
console (`/dev/ttyGS0`). The service enables `serial-getty@ttyGS0.service`.
With `CONFIG_U_SERIAL_CONSOLE=y`, the gadget can also be a kernel log console.
Select it with `console=ttyGS0,115200n8 console=tty0`; keep tty0 last so
userspace /dev/console remains the screen and does not depend on a USB host.
Verify ttyGS0 is enabled in /proc/consoles, not only that its ConfigFS
console attribute is 1. Printk replay is bounded by the gadget FIFO and
does not guarantee a complete early boot log. The service runs from
sysinit.target, before local filesystems are mounted.

If the same Mi Pad 2 ACM gadget is already bound (for example by an
initramfs), starting the helper preserves its UDC binding and connection.
This does not change explicit service stop or suspend detach behavior.

On Windows it appears as **Mi Pad 2 USB Serial**, normally a `COM` port. Use
115200 8N1. This is a fallback debug path when Wi-Fi is unavailable.

The package does not install, replace, or configure Wi-Fi or Bluetooth
firmware/modules. It replaces the old Mi Pad 2 USB Ethernet gadget because a
single UDC cannot bind both legacy `g_ether` and the ACM function.


## Suspend / S0ix

The USB serial service is stopped immediately before system sleep and restored
after resume only when it was active beforehand. This temporarily detaches the
configfs ACM gadget from the Cherry Trail DWC3 UDC so the OTG block is free to
enter a low-power state instead of remaining in D0 throughout s2idle. The
gadget configuration itself is preserved and the serial console returns after
resume.
